INT. FAMILY KITCHEN - FLOOR ABOVE A LAUNDROMAT - EVENING

A small table. A pot of boiled dumplings. A cracked ceiling fan. Rain on the window.

INES, forties, stands at the stove. Her son BOYD, twelve, slumps at the table with his phone. LITTLE TUAN, six, kneels on a chair, drawing a dog.

BOYD
Why does Aunt Seraphina always call during dinner?

INES
Because she knows I'll answer.

The phone on the counter rings. Ines wipes her hands, answers, sets it on speaker.

SERAPHINA (V.O.)
(tinny)
Ines? Is that you? Are the children eating?

INES
We're all here, Seraphina.

SERAPHINA (V.O.)
Good. Boyd, are you wearing the scarf I knitted?

BOYD
It's June, Aunt Sera.

SERAPHINA (V.O.)
Scarves are for every month.

TUAN
Aunt Sera, I drew a dog.

SERAPHINA (V.O.)
Does the dog have a scarf?

TUAN
He does now.

He scribbles one in red crayon. The front door downstairs bangs.

GIDEON, fifties, wet to the skin, an umbrella turned inside out, trudges into the kitchen. Ines stiffens. Boyd lowers his phone.

GIDEON
Sorry I'm late. The bus broke down.

INES
You said you'd be home Friday.

GIDEON
It's Friday.

INES
It's Sunday, Gideon.

A silence. Seraphina's voice trickles from the phone.

SERAPHINA (V.O.)
Gideon? Is that Gideon? Put him on!

Nobody moves. Tuan holds up the dog.

TUAN
Papa, look.
